In dermoscopy, squamous cell carcinoma often shows a pink to red background with thick white-yellow keratin scale or crust, irregular linear and glomerular vessels, focal ulceration, shiny white lines, and variable hyperkeratotic or exophytic nodular patterns.
Dermoscopic appearance often shows erythematous to pink background with scaling or keratin masses, white structureless areas, central crust or ulceration, and polymorphous vascular patterns (glomerular, dotted, hairpin vessels); variation id: 2.
Dermoscopic images typically show a scaly or crusted surface with central keratin, white structureless areas, irregular linear‑irregular and hairpin or glomerular vessels, variable brownish pigmentation, and occasional ulceration; variation 3 emphasizes prominent keratin plug and vascularity.
Under dermoscopy, squamous cell carcinoma often appears as a crusted, scaly lesion with central hyperkeratotic white yellow mass, irregular white structureless areas, pink to red background, and polymorphous vessels including dotted, linear irregular, or glomerular patterns, variation 4.
Squamous cell carcinoma dermoscopic images often show central yellowish-white keratin masses or structureless zones, overlying scales or crust, polymorphous vascular patterns (coiled, hairpin or dotted vessels), focal ulceration and irregular red or pink background, with variable pigmentation.
often displays an irregular, scaly, keratotic plaque with central ulceration, polymorphous vessels (hairpin, glomerular, dotted), white scales, surface crusting, and variable pigmentation ranging from pink to brown to targetoid darker areas.
dermoscopic image shows an erythematous, scaly plaque with central yellow-white keratin mass or crust, irregular white structureless areas, polymorphous vessels (hairpin, glomerular, linear-irregular), white circles and focal ulceration; pigmentation varies.
On dermoscopy, squamous cell carcinoma appears as a scaly, keratotic pink‑red plaque with irregular polymorphous vessels (glomerular, hairpin, linear‑irregular), white structureless areas, crusting, focal brown dots, and Variation 8 shows pronounced surface keratosis.
Dermoscopy of squamous cell carcinoma commonly reveals an irregular central keratinous plug or scale, crust or erosion, white structureless areas, focal keratin pearls, and polymorphous vessels (hairpin, dotted, glomerular) on erythematous or brownish background.
Dermoscopy shows an irregular, scaly erythematous plaque with often prominent surface and polymorphous vessels (dotted, glomerular, hairpin), central keratinous crust or ulceration, white-to-yellow keratin masses, and variable pigmentation or scaling patterns (variation 10).
